artifact.self_contained_problem:
Pulsars are rapidly rotating, strongly magnetized neutron stars observed as periodic sources of electromagnetic radiation, and they are known to interact with their surroundings through relativistic winds, high-energy emission, supernova-remnant injection, and gravitational interactions with companion stars. Globular clusters are dense, gravitationally bound stellar systems in which frequent stellar encounters, exchange interactions, and tidal captures produce a high incidence of binary systems and recycled (millisecond) pulsars; the Galactic field, by contrast, consists of comparatively low-density stellar populations in which neutron stars evolve under largely isolated conditions following their progenitor supernovae.

Characterize quantitatively and qualitatively the role that pulsars play in cosmic evolution and in stellar dynamics, addressing in particular:

(1) the channels through which pulsars contribute to the energetic, chemical, and dynamical evolution of their host environments (e.g., feedback from pulsar winds and relativistic outflows, contributions to cosmic-ray populations, heating of the interstellar/intracluster medium, and gravitational influence on neighboring stars);

(2) how these contributions differ between (a) the dense, interaction-rich environment of globular clusters, where dynamical formation channels (tidal capture, exchange encounters, three-body interactions) shape the pulsar binary population and recycling history, and (b) the Galactic field, where pulsar populations are dominated by primordial binary evolution and isolated spin-down;

(3) the resulting differences in observable population statistics, such as the relative abundance of millisecond versus normal pulsars, binary companion distributions, spatial and kinematic distributions, and spin/magnetic-field evolution, as a function of environment.

Provide a unified description that links the local micro-physics of pulsar emission and binary interaction to the macroscopic evolution of the host stellar system.
